            name: "American Plum",
            category: "American plum species for Connecticut",
            description: "Hardiness: Zone 3. One inch fruit in red, orange and yellow. Grows 15 - 20 ft. White flowers. Red fall color. Suckers freely Great pollinator for hybrid Japanese-American plums. Source table names this as American plum (Prunus americana)"
        },
        {
            type: "Japanese Plum",
            name: "Elephant Heart",
            category: "Hybrid Japanese-American plum varieties for Connecticut",
            harvest_time_unparsed: "Ripens in September.",
            description: "Hardiness: Zones 5 - 8. Developed by Luther Burbank in 1929. Dark red flesh, flavorful, freestone. Fresh or for canning. Ripens in September. Pollinate with Redheart, Starking Delicious, Shiro or Ozark Premier"
        },
        {
            type: "Japanese Plum",
            name: "Gracious",
            category: "Hybrid Japanese-American plum varieties for Connecticut",
            description: "Hardiness: Zone 3 or 4. Developed at the North Dakota Northern Great Plains Experiment Station. Cross of Japanese and American plums. Oval yellow-orange fruit that are firm, sweet and juicy. Freestone. Fresh, dried or preserved. Upright, spreading habit. (Fedco)"
        },
        {
            type: "Japanese Plum",
            name: "Methley",
            category: "Hybrid Japanese-American plum varieties for Connecticut",
            harvest_time_unparsed: "Early ripening.",
            description: "Hardiness: Zones 5 - 9. Early ripening. Round, sweet, juicy purple fruit with a red blush. Heat tolerant. Disease resistant. Partially self-fruitful but better with Shiro or Ozark Premier"
        },
        {
            type: "Japanese Plum",
            name: "Ozark Premier",
            category: "Hybrid Japanese-American plum varieties for Connecticut",
            harvest_time_unparsed: "Ripens early to mid-August.",
            description: "Hardiness: Zones 5 - 9. Bright red fruit with a tough skin. Yellow flesh, slightly tart. Clingstone. Ripens early to mid-August. Pollinate with Starking Delicious, Methley, Shiro, Burbank or Red Ace"
        },
        {
            type: "Japanese Plum",
            name: "Santa Rosa",
            category: "Hybrid Japanese-American plum varieties for Connecticut",
            harvest_time_unparsed: "Mid-season.",
            description: "Hardiness: Zones 5 -9. Developed by Luther Burbank, introduced in 1906. Large, sweet and juicy red fleshed, clingstone plums. Vigorous tree to 12 feet. Mid-season. Somewhat self-fruitful but best with another Japanese plum variety for pollination. References say slow to bear, sometimes produces small crops and requires above average skill to grow. Upright growth habit. High quality dessert plum or use for canning and freezing"
        },
        {
            type: "Japanese Plum",
            name: "Satsuma",
            category: "Hybrid Japanese-American plum varieties for Connecticut",
            harvest_time_unparsed: "Midseason ripening.",
            description: "Hardiness: Zone 4 - 9. Medium red Japanese plum. Sweet, hardy, productive. Midseason ripening. Needs another Japanese plum for pollination"
        },
        {
            type: "Japanese Plum",
            name: "Shiro",
            category: "Hybrid Japanese-American plum varieties for Connecticut",
            harvest_time_unparsed: "An early ripening, yellow round plum that is mild and sweet.",
            description: "Hardiness: Zones 5 - 8. An early ripening, yellow round plum that is mild and sweet. Good for fresh eating, preserving or cooking. Reliable bearer. Grows 12 to 15 feet high. Easy to grow, vigorous and productive. Grown since 1899. Needs another Japanese plum for cross-pollination and is a good pollinator for Santa Rosa,Methley and Satsuma. Spreading growth habit. Plan on thinning the fruit"
        },
        {
            type: "Japanese Plum",
            name: "South Dakota",
            category: "Hybrid Japanese-American plum varieties for Connecticut",
            harvest_time_unparsed: "Late season.",
            description: "Hardiness: To zone 3. Developed by M. E. Hansen at the South Dakota Agriculture Exp. Stn. in early 1900's and later brought by one of his students to the University of Minnesota where it was introduced. Parentage unknown. It is a good pollinator because it blooms over a long period of time. Small to medium size yellow fleshed, freestone fruit with tough yellow skin sporting a reddish blush. Late season"
        },
        {
            type: "Japanese Plum",
            name: "Superior",
            category: "Hybrid Japanese-American plum varieties for Connecticut",
            description: "Hardiness: Zone 3 or 4. Another hybrid with large, dark red, dessert plums. Sweet yellow flesh. Clingstone. One grower says some resistance to brown rot. Introduced by U. Minn. In 1933. Vigorous tree. Precocious producer. Source table names this as Superior (P. salicina 'Burbank' x Kaga) (P. americana x P. simonii)"
        },
        {
            type: "Japanese Plum",
            name: "Toka",
            category: "Hybrid Japanese-American plum varieties for Connecticut",
            harvest_time_unparsed: "Upright plants with fruit ripening mid-season.",
            description: "Hardiness: Zone 4 - 8. Reddish bronze fruit on 10 to 14 foot trees. Easy to grow with sweet, spicy, medium size fruit used for fresh eating or canning. Upright plants with fruit ripening mid-season. Developed by Dr. Hansen at the South Dakota Experiment Station. Says hybrid of Japanese and European types and needs to be pollinated with another hybrid like 'Pipestone'"
        },
        {
            type: "Japanese Plum",
            name: "Waneta",
            category: "Hybrid Japanese-American plum varieties for Connecticut",
            harvest_time_unparsed: "Midseason.",
            description: "Hardiness: To zone 3. Midseason. Released by M.E. Hansen from SD Exp. Station. Named after a Yanktonai youth who rose to fame during the War of 1812 and went on to become a chief. Two-inch maroon-skinned, orange fleshed, slightly tart juicy fruit. Open, spreading growth habit. Blooms early. Source table names this as Waneta (P. salicina 'Apple' x P. americana 'Terry')"
        },
        {
