            description: "Hardiness: Zones 5 - 8. Large, dark blue fruit. Ripens late in season. Productive. 10 to 14 feet high and wide. Pollinator recommended. President x Emily cross. Use fresh or for cooking"
        },
        {
            type: "Euro Plum",
            name: "Fellenberg",
            category: "European plum varieties for Connecticut",
            harvest_time_unparsed: "Ripens in early to mid-September.",
            description: "Hardiness: Zones 5 - 8. A German type prune plum. Self-fertile. Ripens in early to mid-September. Purple skinned, yellow fleshed fruit. Very productive with a spreading growth habit. Used for fresh, canning and preserves"
        },
        {
            type: "Euro Plum",
            name: "Green Gage",
            category: "European plum varieties for Connecticut",
            harvest_time_unparsed: "Ripens in August.",
            description: "Hardiness: Zones 5 - 7. Greenish yellow fruit. Ripens in August. Freestone. Good for cooking and fresh eating. Self-fertile. Good pollinator. Old European plum known by many names. Brought to France by Queen Claude and called the 'Reine Claude'. Later plum trees imported by an English family and as they had no name tags the gardener named it 'Green Gage' after the owners. Several sports including the Golden Transparent Gage and the Reine Red"
        },
        {
            type: "Euro Plum",
            name: "Italian",
            category: "European plum varieties for Connecticut",
            harvest_time_unparsed: "Ripens mid-season.",
            description: "Hardiness: Zones 5 - 8. Dark purple skin with sweet yellow flash. Somewhat self-fertile, freestone, upright growth habit. Bears young. Good pollinator. 10 to 14 feet. Ripens mid-season. Says aka Fellenberg and was replaced in East by hardier Stanley. Good for dessert, drying, canning and cooking. Source table names this as Italian Plum"
        },
        {
            type: "Euro Plum",
            name: "Polly",
            category: "European plum varieties for Connecticut",
            description: "Hardiness: Zones 5 - 9. Large, purple, oval fruit for fresh eating. Upright-spreading tree of medium vigor. Productive and self-fertile"
        },
        {
            type: "Euro Plum",
            name: "President",
            category: "European plum varieties for Connecticut",
            harvest_time_unparsed: "Ripens late in season.",
            description: "Hardiness: Zones 5 - 8. Large, blue prune plum. Vigorous. Productive. Freestone. Ripens late in season. Resistant to black knot and bacterial spot. 10 - 14 feet. Use fresh, canned or in preserves. Pollinator recommended. Vigorous, upright tree"
        },
